WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:11.010
In 1964 President Johnson, declared a
national War on Poverty. So how we done?

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:16.830
By one metric, not too well. In 1966, when
the last major War on Poverty programs

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:21.480
were coming online, the official census
poverty rate stood at 14.7 percent.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.170
Since then, the government
has spent about 20 trillion dollars on

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:29.460
these programs. And with all this money,
we've only dropped the rate by about one

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:33.809
percentage point, to 13.5 percent. Now, the official poverty rate is

00:00:33.809 --> 00:00:37.980
an imperfect measurement. It excludes
some government programs and the modern

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.820
comforts that capitalism and innovation
have brought to everyone, up and down the

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.510
income distribution. But America wasn't
just trying to make poverty a little

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:50.340
less miserable to endure. We were trying
to make poverty more escapable. If more

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.750
Americans had been able to escape
poverty and earn their own success, we

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.739
would see a lot more progress in the
official poverty rate. That's why the War

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:03.600
on Poverty has been a moral failure. We
need to make poverty not just easier to

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.600
bear, but easier to escape.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.600
To learn more about how to help Americans escape poverty, check the links in the description below.

